2026-06-30 15:42:24,043 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-fe167488-c029-4a4e-91b4-33067dfceeb1', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nF2203_chenxi_086.raw\nF2203_chenxi_087.raw\nF2203_chenxi_088.raw\nF2203_chenxi_089.raw\nF2203_chenxi_090.raw\nF2203_chenxi_091.raw\nF2203_chenxi_092.raw\nF2203_chenxi_093.raw\nF2203_chenxi_253.raw\nF2203_chenxi_254.raw\nF2203_chenxi_258.raw\nF2203_chenxi_259.raw\nF2203_chenxi_264.raw\nF2203_chenxi_265.raw\nF2203_chenxi_269.raw\nF2203_chenxi_270.raw\nF2209_chenxi_188.raw\nF2209_chenxi_189.raw\nF2209_chenxi_191.raw\nF2209_chenxi_192.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nA photo-caged cross-linker for identifying protein-protein interactions\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nIn recent years cross-linking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) as a tool to study protein-protein interactions (PPIs) has undergone significant improvements, which were mainly propelled by the emergence of novel crosslinkers and the development and implementation of advanced analysis workflows. Despite these recent advancements, addressing PPIs in the cell by XL-MS remains a substantial challenge. Some of the current limitations are linked to a comparable poor membrane permeability of most crosslinkers and their limited efficiency to traverse the cellular membrane. To address this problem, we have designed and synthesized a novel crosslinker based on the chemical o-nitrobenzylester (o-NBE) group, which enhances the hydrophobic properties of the cross-linker.\n\n[METHODS]\n100 µg of Bovine Serum Albumin (BSA) at a concentration of 1 µg/µl was mixed with the crosslinker o-Nitrosobenzaldehyde (o-NBE) at a final concentration of 2mM. The reaction was allowed to proceed over time intervals of 0 minutes, 10 minutes, 20 minutes and 30 minutes, using 365nm UV light (Thorlabs M365L2, 190 mW (min). Following UV-light activation, the crosslinked samples were incubated at 37°C under continuous shaking at 650 rpm for 3 hours in a Thermomixer (Eppendorf) before sodium triacetoxyhydroborate (STAB) at a concentration of 10 mM was added for 20 minutes to quench the reaction by reduction.  In-gel digest was performed as previously described. The crosslinked samples were separated by SDS-PAGE and stained overnight. Protein bands were excised into small segments of around 1 cm² and destained with a 50/50 (v/v) solution of acetonitrile (ACN) and 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate (ABi) and incubated for 15 minutes at 37°C at 650 rpm. Subsequently, protein bands were reduced in 100 mM NH₄HCO3, 10 mM DTT for 30 min at 56° C, alkylated in 100 mM NH₄HCO3, 55 mM iodoacetamide for 20 min at 23° C in the dark, and digested with 13ng/µl trypsin (Promega, sequencing grade) in 10 mM 100 mM NH₄HCO3, 10% ACN for 2 h on ice, followed by 18 h at 37° C. Samples were extracted and dried using a SpeedVac. Dried peptides were re-suspended in 0.1% TFA for 15 min at 37° C and adjusted to pH <3 with TFA. The peptides were loaded onto Pierce C18 SpinTip columns (Thermo, 10 µg) and desalted according to protocol. Crosslinked peptides were separated on an EASY-nLC 1200 system (Thermo Scientific) at a flow rate of 300 nl/min over a 60 min gradient elution (5 % acetonitrile in 0.1 % formic acid for 4 min, 5 % - 35 % acetonitrile in 0.1% formic acid in 55 min, 35 % - 80 % acetonitrile in 1 min) and measured on a Orbitrap Fusion Tribrid mass spectrometer (Thermo Electron, San Jose, CA). Full scan mass spectra were acquired in the Orbitrap at a resolution of 120,000, a scan range of 400–1600 m/z, and a maximum injection time of 50 ms. Precursor ions with charge states 3-8 were fragmented by CID at 35 % collision in a data dependent mode. The exclusion duration of dynamic exclusion is 60s and spectra were analyzed in the ion trap with rapid scan rate.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nBos taurus (bovine)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
