2026-06-30 15:42:38,295 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-e0da9737-0d18-463d-b258-a0b042586b0b', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the mass spectrometry acquisition.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: instrument\nsdrf_column: comment[instrument]\nontology: MS:1000031 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Mass spectrometer instrument make and model.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology. If multiple instruments, return a list.\n  Common instruments (name → accession):\n    Orbitrap Astral → MS:1003356\n    Orbitrap Exploris 480 → MS:1003028\n    Orbitrap Eclipse → MS:1003349\n    Orbitrap Fusion Lumos → MS:1002732\n    Orbitrap Fusion → MS:1002416\n    Q Exactive HF-X → MS:1002877\n    Q Exactive HF → MS:1002523\n    Q Exactive Plus → MS:1002634\n    Q Exactive → MS:1001911\n    LTQ Orbitrap Velos → MS:1001742\n    LTQ Orbitrap XL → MS:1000556\n    timsTOF → MS:1003005\n    timsTOF Pro → MS:1003230\n    TripleTOF 6600 → MS:1002583\n  Format: NT=<instrument name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: proteomics data acquisition method\nsdrf_column: comment[proteomics data acquisition method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000659\ndescription: Mass spectrometry data acquisition method.\ninstructions: For XL-MS this is nearly always DDA. Use:\n  NT=data-dependent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000041 — for DDA/shotgun\n  NT=data-independent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000449 — for DIA (rare in XL-MS)\n  NT=targeted acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000450 — for SRM/PRM\n  Default to DDA if not stated.\n\nterm: dissociation method\nsdrf_column: comment[dissociation method]\nontology: MS:1000044 (PSI-MS children)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nA photo-caged cross-linker for identifying protein-protein interactions\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nIn recent years cross-linking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) as a tool to study protein-protein interactions (PPIs) has undergone significant improvements, which were mainly propelled by the emergence of novel crosslinkers and the development and implementation of advanced analysis workflows. Despite these recent advancements, addressing PPIs in the cell by XL-MS remains a substantial challenge. Some of the current limitations are linked to a comparable poor membrane permeability of most crosslinkers and their limited efficiency to traverse the cellular membrane. To address this problem, we have designed and synthesized a novel crosslinker based on the chemical o-nitrobenzylester (o-NBE) group, which enhances the hydrophobic properties of the cross-linker.\n\n[METHODS]\n100 µg of Bovine Serum Albumin (BSA) at a concentration of 1 µg/µl was mixed with the crosslinker o-Nitrosobenzaldehyde (o-NBE) at a final concentration of 2mM. The reaction was allowed to proceed over time intervals of 0 minutes, 10 minutes, 20 minutes and 30 minutes, using 365nm UV light (Thorlabs M365L2, 190 mW (min). Following UV-light activation, the crosslinked samples were incubated at 37°C under continuous shaking at 650 rpm for 3 hours in a Thermomixer (Eppendorf) before sodium triacetoxyhydroborate (STAB) at a concentration of 10 mM was added for 20 minutes to quench the reaction by reduction.  In-gel digest was performed as previously described. The crosslinked samples were separated by SDS-PAGE and stained overnight. Protein bands were excised into small segments of around 1 cm² and destained with a 50/50 (v/v) solution of acetonitrile (ACN) and 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate (ABi) and incubated for 15 minutes at 37°C at 650 rpm. Subsequently, protein bands were reduced in 100 mM NH₄HCO3, 10 mM DTT for 30 min at 56° C, alkylated in 100 mM NH₄HCO3, 55 mM iodoacetamide for 20 min at 23° C in the dark, and digested with 13ng/µl trypsin (Promega, sequencing grade) in 10 mM 100 mM NH₄HCO3, 10% ACN for 2 h on ice, followed by 18 h at 37° C. Samples were extracted and dried using a SpeedVac. Dried peptides were re-suspended in 0.1% TFA for 15 min at 37° C and adjusted to pH <3 with TFA. The peptides were loaded onto Pierce C18 SpinTip columns (Thermo, 10 µg) and desalted according to protocol. Crosslinked peptides were separated on an EASY-nLC 1200 system (Thermo Scientific) at a flow rate of 300 nl/min over a 60 min gradient elution (5 % acetonitrile in 0.1 % formic acid for 4 min, 5 % - 35 % acetonitrile in 0.1% formic acid in 55 min, 35 % - 80 % acetonitrile in 1 min) and measured on a Orbitrap Fusion Tribrid mass spectrometer (Thermo Electron, San Jose, CA). Full scan mass spectra were acquired in the Orbitrap at a resolution of 120,000, a scan range of 400–1600 m/z, and a maximum injection time of 50 ms. Precursor ions with charge states 3-8 were fragmented by CID at 35 % collision in a data dependent mode. The exclusion duration of dynamic exclusion is 60s and spectra were analyzed in the ion trap with rapid scan rate.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
